window.docContents[179].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Mathematical-Transition-at-Shock-Formation--Why-Entropy-Conditions-Are-Needed":{"contents":"Consider Burgers's equation  with Riemann data\n for  and  for . Its entropy solution is\na shock moving at speed :\n\n\n\nBut the same equation with the reversed Riemann data  for\n and  for  shows why the weak identity is\ninsufficient. The entropy solution is the expansion fan\n for , but the nonphysical expansion shock jumping\nfrom 0 to 1 at speed  is also a distributional weak solution. The\nspeed is fixed, not arbitrary: Rankine–Hugoniot gives\n.\n\n\n\nThe figure places two mechanisms side by side in one initial profile: a\ndescending ramp compresses, while a separate upward jump opens into a fan.\nBefore the ramp breaks, its characteristic denominator is the same one\nchecked in the affine Burgers\nexample. At the breaking time that denominator vanishes. Continuing the\npicture past that time requires the weak and entropy formulations, with the\ntwo-state Riemann profiles as explicit models.\n\nThe entropy condition resolves this ambiguity by imposing a directionality on\nshocks. The Lax condition  requires that characteristics\nenter the shock from both sides, modeling compression. For the displayed\nconvex Burgers jump, this selects the same branch as the Kružkov inequalities\n for\nevery constant . With this mathematical-entropy convention, the spatial\nintegral of the entropy is nonincreasing when boundary fluxes vanish. Physical\nentropy is often written with the opposite sign.\n\nThe physical origin of this condition is the vanishing-viscosity limit. The\nequation  is\nparabolic for . Under the standard scalar-flux and data\nhypotheses, its solutions converge as  to the entropy\nsolution; the proof of convergence needs compactness and stability estimates\nbeyond the formal perturbation.\n\nEvans (2010, §3.4, pp. 135–161, and Ch. 11, pp. 609–653) develops the\ntheory;  and \ngave the foundational entropy conditions.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws\u0009The Mathematical Transition at Shock Formation","header":"7.5.2.1.1. Why Entropy Conditions Are Needed","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Mathematical-Transition-at-Shock-Formation--Why-Entropy-Conditions-Are-Needed"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves","header":"7.2.4. Nonlinear Wave Equations","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.6. Nonlinear Heat Singularity Profiles","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Nonlinear-Heat-Singularity-Profiles"}});